1) 先确认是否是计划内发布：查看变更记录与 shell 历史；\n2) 非计划:
1) First confirm whether this was a planned release by checking change records and shell history;
2) If unplanned, run journalctl -u httpd --since '-30min' and tail -200 error_log to determine whether it was a segfault, an OOM, or a configuration error;
3) For an OOM kill, confirm with dmesg -T | grep -i 'killed process' and lower MaxRequestWorkers based on real per-worker memory;
4) For repeated crashes, keep core dumps (CoreDumpDirectory) and bisect by disabling third-party modules one at a time.

1) 在 access_log 的 LogFormat 里确认已带 %D（微秒耗:
1) Confirm the access_log LogFormat includes %D (microsecond duration), then run awk '{print $NF, $7}' access_log | sort -rn | head -20 to find the slowest URLs;
2) If the slow URLs are dynamic requests, move on to the backend (PHP-FPM slowlog, application logs, database slow queries);
3) If static assets are slow, check disk IO and whether sendfile or caching was accidentally disabled;
4) While investigating, apply mod_ratelimit or degrade that URL so workers are not held indefinitely.

1) 执行 curl -s 'http://127.0.0.1/server-s:
1) Run curl -s 'http://127.0.0.1/server-status?auto' and compare BusyWorkers against the open_slot headroom in the Scoreboard;
2) Use tail -n 10000 access_log | awk to rank URLs by count and latency, and decide whether this is genuine traffic growth or one slow endpoint holding workers;
3) For traffic growth, scale out instances or raise MaxRequestWorkers (recalculating ServerLimit against memory);
4) For a slow endpoint, fix the backend first and apply mod_reqtimeout or rate limiting to that URL so a single endpoint cannot drain the whole process pool.

